<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SPDX-License-Identifier: PolyForm-Noncommercial-1.0.0

  THE PLATFORM'S OWN COPY OF THE RULE THIS APPLICATION ALREADY ENFORCES TWICE.

  core/net_post.cpp's netPostAllowed() refuses anything but https, with one
  exception for plain http to a loopback address; Net.java refuses the same
  thing again on its own terms. Android has a third, independent say: since
  Android 9 an application that targets API 28 or later may not send cleartext
  at all unless a file like this one permits it, and with no file the platform
  answers "Cleartext HTTP traffic to 127.0.0.1 not permitted" - which is
  exactly what it did, and which is how this file came to exist.

  WHAT THIS PERMITS, and it is deliberately the narrowest thing that works:
  cleartext to 127.0.0.1, ::1 and localhost. Nothing else. The base
  configuration below keeps the platform's own default - cleartext refused -
  for every other destination, so the usage endpoint, the crash endpoint and
  anything a later slice reaches are all still https-or-nothing, enforced by
  the operating system rather than only by our code.

  A loopback address never leaves the device: there is no network in between
  to collect anything, which is the entire reason all three gates make this one
  exception and no other.
-->
<network-security-config>

    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">::1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
    </domain-config>

    <!--
      A DEBUGGABLE BUILD MAY TRUST A CERTIFICATE THE PERSON TESTING IT
      INSTALLED, AND A SHIPPED ONE MAY NOT. The platform enforces that split
      itself: everything inside <debug-overrides> applies only when the
      INSTALLED package carries android:debuggable, which is exactly the flag
      core/net_post.cpp already reads to decide whether this build may post at
      all (readDebuggableFlag). A release APK ignores this block entirely -
      aapt2 keeps it in the resource, and the platform does not consult it - so
      the shipped application still trusts the system store and nothing else.

      WHY IT IS NEEDED. Both uploads are https, and the crash endpoint's
      certificate is a public one - which means there is no way to watch a real
      upload arrive at a real server on a development machine: the server an
      emulator can reach is the host at 10.0.2.2, and no public CA will name
      that address. Testing it over cleartext instead tests a different
      transport (HttpURLConnection, not HttpsURLConnection) and therefore
      proves nothing about the one that ships. With this block, the host can
      present a certificate for 10.0.2.2 from a CA installed on the device, and
      the SAME code path that posts to foxsdr.com is the one under test. See
      tools/android_tls_front.py, which is the other half of that arrangement.

      THE SYSTEM ANCHORS ARE LISTED FIRST AND ON PURPOSE: a trust-anchors block
      REPLACES the default set rather than adding to it, so omitting them would
      leave a debuggable build unable to verify any public certificate - which
      would silently turn every real-endpoint test into a failure whose cause
      is this file.

      tests/test_android_network_config.cpp holds all of this: cleartext
      refused by default, the loopback exception unchanged, and - the assertion
      that matters - a user trust anchor only ever inside this block.
    -->
    <debug-overrides>
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="user" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </debug-overrides>

</network-security-config>
